  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Dakota has recorded $595M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government issued 3,181 awards in the state, with an average award size of $187K. Obligations are down 26.0% year over year. Historical annual obligations show variability: FY21 was $624M, FY22 was $1.2B, FY23 was $1B, FY24 was $1.3B, FY25 was $804M, and FY26 is $595M. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $256M in obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $117M and the Department of the Interior with $99.7M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $137M, and Computer Facilities Management Services follows with $68.6M. KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor with $68.3M in obligations, and CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC are the next largest vendors with $42.5M and $40.7M respectively. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in South Dakota changed recently?",
        "body": "Total obligations in South Dakota are $595M for FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 26.0% year over year. Annual obligations have ranged from $624M in FY21 to $1.3B in FY24, with FY25 at $804M and FY26 at $595M. The award count is 3,181 and the average award size is $187K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries lead federal spending in South Dakota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buying agency with $256M in obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $117M and the Department of the Interior with $99.7M. KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor with $68.3M, and CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC and FEDERAL CONTRACTING INC follow with $42.5M and $40.7M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industries with $137M, followed by Computer Facilities Management Services with $68.6M."
      }
    ]
